  "text": "Shamanism shows up in many lands, yet people often call it an arctic faith. The term shaman springs from Tungus speech in southern Siberia. Some scholars see shamanic threads in Celtic religion. That link may not reach back to the arctic peoples at all. Two points join the two. First, the poet served as a seer who changed shape. Second, a second world stood apart from ours yet joined it. The Song of Amairgin lets the poet be a sea wave or a sun tear. A shaman, drumming and dancing, might turn into a bear or another beast. Satire kept the poet's folk balanced, much as the arctic shaman healed. People often set the Celtic Otherworld beside the Dreamtime. Parts of shamanism may be missing among the Celts. No one knows if they ate mind-altering plants. Proof that they used ale for such ends is firm. Arctic hysteria, an ailment that usually opens initiation, had no Celtic form. Okinawan nuru and Korean mudang shamans train for years. They break down seldom. So the missing hysteria does not rule out Celtic shamanism. Experts still split on the matter. Some insist the term shaman belongs to Asian faiths. Some academics doubt any rebuilding of lost religion. Cheap commercial shamanism rests on little theory or spirit. Even so, groups still sell themselves as Celtic shamans.",
